# Internationalization

Rules for user-facing copy and locale switching. Follow global rules in `global.mdc`.

## Current setup

This app uses **`@nuxtjs/i18n`**. English is the only locale. Add a language by copying that pattern. Do not add a second i18n library or a custom plugin.

| Item | Value |
|------|-------|
| Module | `@nuxtjs/i18n` in `nuxt.config.ts` |
| Messages | `i18n/locales/{code}.ts` |
| Default | `en` |
| Strategy | `no_prefix` (locale is not in the URL) |
| Browser detect | Off, until more than one locale exists |
| Switch | `app/layouts/default.vue` calls `setLocale` |

```ts
locales: [
  {
    code: 'en',
    language: 'en-US',
    name: 'English',
    dir: 'ltr',
    file: 'en.ts',
  },
]
```

`langDir` is `locales`, resolved under the `i18n/` directory.

## Message files

One TypeScript file per locale. Every file exports the same key tree. English is the source.

```ts
export default {
  home: {
    title: 'Nuxt Template',
    description: 'Start building from this page.',
  },
  language: {
    label: 'Language',
  },
}
```

- Nest keys by screen or feature: `home.title`, `language.label`.
- Use camelCase key segments.
- Keep keys stable. Renaming a key means updating every locale file and every `$t()` call.
- Write full sentences in the locale file. Do not glue translated fragments together in the template.
- Leave brand and product names untranslated unless the copy asks for a translation.

## Using translations

In templates, use `$t`:

```vue
<h1>{{ $t('home.title') }}</h1>
```

In script, use `useI18n()`:

```ts
const { t } = useI18n()
const title = computed(() => t('home.title'))
```

- Translate every string a person sees: labels, buttons, empty states, validation, and page titles.
- Pass dynamic values as named params: `$t('items.count', { count })`.
- Format dates and numbers with the i18n date and number APIs when a locale needs a different format.

## Language switch

The switch reads `locales` from `useI18n()` and changes locale with `setLocale`. Locale names in the control come from the config `name` (`English`), not from a translated message, so the language name stays recognizable.

When you add a locale:

1. Add `i18n/locales/{code}.ts` with the same keys as `en.ts`.
2. Register `{ code, language, name, dir, file }` in `nuxt.config.ts`.
3. Set `dir` to `ltr` or `rtl`.
4. Keep `strategy: 'no_prefix'` unless the product needs the locale in the path.
5. Turn on `detectBrowserLanguage` only after at least two locales exist.

Set `dir` on the document from the active locale when a right-to-left language is added. Do not hardcode `ltr` in every page.

## Adding a locale

```ts
{
  code: 'fa',
  language: 'fa-IR',
  name: 'فارسی',
  dir: 'rtl',
  file: 'fa.ts',
}
```

`language` is the BCP 47 tag (`en-US`, `fa-IR`). `code` is the short id used by `setLocale` and message files.

## Do

- Use `@nuxtjs/i18n` and `i18n/locales/{code}.ts`.
- Keep one shared key tree. English is the complete source.
- Use `$t` / `t()` for user-facing copy.
- Switch locale with `setLocale` from `useI18n()`.
- Show each locale's own `name` in the language control.
- Set `dir` on the locale entry (`ltr` or `rtl`).
- Add the new key to every existing locale file in the same change.
- Install or upgrade the module with Yarn.

## Don't

- Don't hardcode UI strings in Vue, TS, or SCSS content.
- Don't add vue-i18n by hand or a second i18n plugin beside `@nuxtjs/i18n`.
- Don't build sentences by concatenating translated pieces.
- Don't put the locale code in the URL while `strategy` is `no_prefix`.
- Don't enable browser language detection while only `en` exists.
- Don't store the active locale in your own `localStorage` key. Let the module persist it.
- Don't translate locale names inside message files for the switcher.
- Don't change a key in only one locale file.
- Don't use `process.env` to pick the locale.
- Don't put API paths, DTO field names, or code identifiers through `$t`.
